module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.node_type_shape_prints_applied_head_without_declaration_identity

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

// THE §5 DIAGNOSTIC DEFECT THE INFER LANE MEASURED BUT COULD NOT FIX HONESTLY (#14152 PR-2 work;
// stern-bat-401 ruling, option (A)). Filed so the class is on the ledger and, crucially, so the
// FIX that is being held is held in the open -- with its reason and its retirement route recorded --
// rather than shipped blind or dropped silently.
data node_type_shape_prints_applied_head_without_declaration_identity: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "node_type_shape_prints_applied_head_without_declaration_identity" as NonEmptyStr,
  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: v1.compiler.infer_types node_type_shape renders an APPLIED type by its head NAME and its argument list, and drops the field that makes two applications of the same head distinct -- the declaration the head resolves to. Its applied-head arm prints `Node(<head-name><args>)` off node.name; the Node's declaration identity is available on the node (the type already carries it, and the module already has readers named node_declaration_identity / node_declaration_file / whole_declaration_carried), but the applied-head arm never reads it. Two applications of one head to different declared carriers therefore print byte-for-byte the same string. CEILING (DESIGN section 5): a renderer that cannot show the field that makes two types differ is not a check -- it is the reason a wrong state looks like a right one. DESIGN section 5, first meta-obligation: the print IS the verdict surface for a human reading a diagnostic; collapsing distinct types to one print ships the collapse to every reader downstream of it.",
    "HARM: every diagnostic whose expected/got text is built from node_type_shape asserts a difference it cannot show and hides the one field that carries it. The self-documented specimen in the same module is `Wrapper<KindB>` vs `Wrapper<KindA>` both printing `Node(Wrapper)`: a reader (or a test asserting on the string) cannot tell the two apart, so a real disagreement between them reads as no disagreement. This is the same silent-wrongness class as every other accepted-but-distinct row, reached from the RENDERER rather than the checker: even when the checker is right, the diagnostic it emits is unreadable.",
    "WHY THIS ROW, AND WHY THE CODE IS NOT IN THIS PR (rung stated honestly; DESIGN section 4b meta-obligation 1 -- the reported rung equals the rung established by EXECUTED evidence; a fix with no discriminating input is a §4b decoration and a §5 ship-blind). The fix (give the applied-head arm its declaration identity, alongside the head name it already prints) was MEASURED to have no authorable in-lane discriminator, reported to the manager, and the manager ruled (A): file the row, hold the code. The measurement, in full: (1) a dag/-rooted claim cannot import v1.compiler.* -- CI floor resolution scans the claim lane under the dag and src/v2 roots only, never src/v1 (documented at dag/test/claim/trait_bound_witness.dag: a prior revision that imported a v1.compiler module failed CI unresolved-import), so no claim can call node_type_shape and assert on its output; (2) the compile instruments that DO execute (compile_dag_diagnostic_census, multi_module_compile_fixture) return rows whose only string fields are diagnostic_class / subject_name / owner_module / declaration_name -- there is no expected/got/message text, so the Wrapper-vs-Wrapper collapse is invisible to them (same class, same subject), and the Wrapper/KindA-KindB specimen is exactly the shape they cannot separate; (3) the Rust unit-test lane (src/v1/tests) is off the required path (gunbc.rung_drop rust_unit_tests_off_the_merge_path), so a Rust-level string assertion would be green-by-vacancy in the same sense that got the #14485 mint-boundary guard deleted -- its only failing arm was an unauthorable red the review refused to accept as coverage. So a code-only change here moves nothing any executed lane can observe: there is no RED that can be authored today, which is the definition of the thing §4b says must not be shipped as a check.",
    "WHAT IS AND IS NOT CLAIMED HERE (do not read this row as the fix landing). CLAIMED: the defect is real, source-grounded (the applied-head arm of node_type_shape reads node.name and ignores node.declaration), and the reason the fix is held is the missing discriminator, not a judgment that the print is fine. NOT CLAIMED: that the fix is done, that it is tested, or that any lane now catches the collapse. The applied-head-arm change lands LATER and ONLY WITH its discriminator -- as its own change, by whoever builds the instrument below -- so the code and the check that proves it arrive together, which is the §4b rule the held change would otherwise violate.",
    "NEXT-RUNG CARRIER -- THE MISSING INSTRUMENT (this is what the row is waiting on). A lane that can OBSERVE a rendered type-shape string from an executing check: the shape two applications of one head to distinct declared carriers print, readable by a claim or a gate-enforced test. Today no such carrier exists (points 1-3 above). Its owner is the instrument lane that owns the census/fixture row types (Charter 4: evidence that actually runs) -- CompileDiagnosticCensusRow carrying no expected/got text is the specific gap. The retirement route (how this row is deleted): build a host intrinsic in the census/fixture family that renders node_type_shape for two constructed applied nodes and exposes the two strings to a dag/ claim (the compile_dag_rust_emit_check pattern is the precedent for a host instrument a claim calls). That intrinsic becomes the discriminator: the claim asserts the two prints DIFFER when the two heads resolve to distinct declarations (RED before the renderer fix, GREEN after), with a same-declaration control asserting they still MATCH (the control that stops the fix from just printing something always-different). When that claim exists and runs on an executing lane, it and the applied-head-arm change land together and this row is deleted in the same change.",
    "LEAVE ALONE WHILE FIXING (the ruling's constraint, recorded so the future fix does not widen). The applied-head arm is one branch; node_type_shape's LEAF arm feeds a consumer in v1.compiler.infer that compares its output to the literal `Primitive(String)`, and there are claims in the claim lane asserting on the Coproduct/Product connective print. Those observe the leaf and connective arms, NOT the applied-head arm, so the applied-head change must not perturb them: the fix adds declaration identity to the `Node(name<args>)` branch only, keeping the leaf arm's `Primitive(...)` text and the connective prints byte-identical. A future author who changes the leaf or connective arms owes those consumers their own measurement, not this row's silence.",
  ],
  evidence: [],
}
